(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-2-anatomy-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"Anatomy Comprehensive Master Exam — Form D","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What do lamellated corpuscles detect, and where are they located?","choices":["Light touch; located in dermal papillae and the root hair plexus","Deep pressure and vibration; located in the reticular layer","Stretch; located in the papillary layer, adjacent to tactile corpuscles","Temperature only; located in the epidermis, near the stratum corneum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lamellated corpuscles detect deep pressure and vibration and are located in the reticular layer. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What are the three main accessory structures of the integumentary system, and how do they develop embryonically?","choices":["Muscles, bones, and glands","Only hair and nails, with no glands","Hair follicles/hair, glands, and nails; all develop through invagination/infolding of the epidermis","Hair, glands, and nails, all developing from the dermis exclusively, with no epidermal contribution"],"answer":2,"exp":"The three main accessory structures are hair follicles and hair, glands, and nails; all of them developed embryonically through invagination/infolding of the epidermis. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What are the hair papillae, hair bulb, and hair matrix?","choices":["Hair papillae contain capillaries and nerves; hair bulb consists of epithelial cells; hair matrix is involved in hair production","Hair papillae are epithelial cells; hair bulb contains capillaries and nerves; hair matrix is unrelated to hair production","Hair papillae are epithelial cells; hair bulb is unrelated to hair growth; hair matrix contains capillaries and nerves","None of these structures relate to hair follicle anatomy, belonging instead to nail formation"],"answer":0,"exp":"The hair papillae contain capillaries and nerves, the hair bulb consists of epithelial cells, and the hair matrix is involved in hair production. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What are the three components of the hair shaft itself, and what type of keratin does each contain?","choices":["Medulla (hard keratin), cortex (soft keratin), and cuticle (soft keratin)","Only two components: cortex and cuticle","Medulla (soft keratin), cortex (hard keratin), and cuticle (hard keratin)","The hair shaft contains no keratin at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hair is composed of the medulla (containing soft keratin), the cortex (containing hard keratin), and the cuticle (containing hard keratin). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What are the three layers of the hair follicle, per the material?","choices":["Medulla, cortex, and cuticle, forming the layers of the hair shaft","Stratum basale, stratum spinosum, and stratum lucidum","Hair root, hair shaft, and hair cuticle","Internal root sheath, external root sheath, and glassy membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"The hair follicle is organized into three layers: the internal root sheath (surrounds the hair root and part of the shaft), the external root sheath (extends from the skin surface to the matrix), and the glassy membrane (a thick basal lamina wrapped by dense connective tissue). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the function of hair at the nostrils, external ear canal, and eyelashes?","choices":["Protect the scalp from UV light","Cushion blows to the head","Prevent entry of foreign objects and insects","Serve as an early warning system to prevent injury"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hair at the nostrils, external ear canal, and eyelashes prevents the entry of foreign objects and insects. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What are the three types of hair described in the material?","choices":["Cortex, cuticle, and follicle","Only vellus and terminal hair","Vellus, intermediate, and terminal","Only intermediate hair types"],"answer":2,"exp":"The three types of hair are vellus, intermediate, and terminal. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What do sebaceous glands produce, and where are sebaceous follicles specifically found?","choices":["Produce a clear, watery secretion; found everywhere on the body's surface except the palms and soles of the hands and feet","Produce sweat for thermoregulation; found only in the axillae, groin, and surrounding apocrine-rich regions","Produce oily lipid (waxy/oily) coating hair and epidermis; sebaceous follicles found only in the face, back, chest, nipples, and male sex organs","Produce keratin; found only in the thick skin of the palms, soles, and fingertip pads of the hands and feet"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sebaceous glands produce an oily lipid (waxy and oily) that coats the hair (typical glands) and epidermis (sebaceous follicles); sebaceous follicles specifically are found only in the face, back, chest, nipples, and male sex organs. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the difference between apocrine and merocrine sweat glands, in terms of location?","choices":["Apocrine glands are confined to the scalp and face; merocrine glands are found in the axillae only","Neither gland type is found on the skin's surface or within its glandular structures","Apocrine glands are found in the axillae, groin, and nipples only; merocrine glands are found everywhere else","Both gland types are found in identical locations across the entire body surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"Apocrine sweat glands are found in the axillae, groin, and nipples only, while merocrine sweat glands are found everywhere else on the body. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What do myoepithelial cells do in sweat glands?","choices":["Produce keratin","Produce melanin pigment","Constrict the gland to discharge secretions","Form the sensory nerve endings of the gland"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sweat glands contain myoepithelial cells, which constrict the gland to discharge secretions. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What are the functions of sweat, per the material?","choices":["Digestion of food particles on the skin surface","Only thermoregulation","Thermoregulation, excretion, and protection","Only excretion of waste"],"answer":2,"exp":"The functions of sweat are thermoregulation, excretion, and protection. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How is control of sebaceous/apocrine gland secretion different from control of merocrine gland secretion?","choices":["Both gland categories have identical control precision, since all are governed uniformly by the hypothalamus","Neither gland category is controlled by the autonomic nervous system, relying instead on local reflexes","Sebaceous/apocrine glands respond only to temperature; merocrine glands respond only to emotional stress","For sebaceous and apocrine glands, the autonomic control is general and regional control is impossible; for merocrine glands, control is more precise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Secretions of exocrine glands are controlled by the autonomic nervous system; for sebaceous and apocrine glands, the effect is general and regional control is impossible, while for merocrine glands, the control is more precise. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the relationship between the bony labyrinth and membranous labyrinth, and what fluids fill each space?","choices":["Both labyrinths are filled with the identical fluid, since perilymph and endolymph have nearly indistinguishable compositions","The bony labyrinth lies within the membranous labyrinth; endolymph surrounds it, and perilymph fills the space inside","The membranous labyrinth lies within the bony labyrinth; perilymph fills the space around the membranous labyrinth, and endolymph fills within the membranous labyrinth","Neither labyrinth contains any fluid; both spaces are instead filled with a gelatinous connective tissue matrix"],"answer":2,"exp":"The membranous labyrinth lies within the bony labyrinth; perilymph fills the space between the bony and membranous labyrinth, while endolymph fills within the membranous labyrinth itself. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the vestibular complex, and what does it detect?","choices":["Part of the middle ear that amplifies sound via the ossicular chain before it reaches the cochlea","Part of the inner ear providing equilibrium sensations by detecting rotation, gravity, and acceleration","Part of the external ear that collects sound waves and funnels them toward the tympanic membrane","A structure detecting only color vision, located within the retina rather than the ear"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vestibular complex is the part of the inner ear that provides equilibrium sensations by detecting rotation, gravity, and acceleration, consisting of the semicircular canals, utricle, saccule, and crista ampularis. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What structures are found within the ampulla of each semicircular canal, and what do hair cells contain?","choices":["A cristae with hair cells; each hair cell contains a kinocilium and stereocilia embedded in the cupula","Statoconia crystals only, with no hair cells, suspended freely within the endolymph of the ampulla","The organ of Corti, resting on the basilar membrane within the ampulla of each canal","Only fluid, with no cellular structures, allowing free endolymph movement through the ampulla"],"answer":0,"exp":"Within each ampulla is a cristae with hair cells; each hair cell contains a kinocilium and stereocilia, which are embedded in gelatinous material called the cupula. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"How does head movement translate into a sensory signal within the semicircular canals?","choices":["Movement of the body has no effect on the endolymph, since the cupula responds only to gravitational pull on otoliths","Movement of the body causes movement of endolymph in the canal, which moves the cupula and hair cells, detected by the brain","The cupula moves independently of endolymph, driven instead by pressure changes transmitted from the middle ear","Perilymph (not endolymph) moves within the semicircular canals, bending the cupula and stimulating hair cells directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"The movement of the body causes movement of fluid (endolymph) in the semicircular canal, which in turn causes movement of the cupula and hair cells, which the brain detects. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What are maculae, and what is an otolith?","choices":["Maculae are found only in the cochlea, arranged along the organ of Corti; otoliths are a specialized nerve fiber in the vestibule","Neither structure is related to equilibrium; maculae instead detect sound frequency, and otoliths regulate endolymph production","Maculae are clusters of photoreceptor cells found in the retina; otoliths are found in the eye, embedded in the vitreous humor","Maculae are clusters of hair cells in the utricle/saccule; an otolith is the gelatinous material plus statoconia (calcium carbonate crystals) covering them"],"answer":3,"exp":"Maculae are clusters of hair cells in the utricle and saccule; an otolith is the gelatinous material and statoconia (calcium carbonate crystals) collectively that embed these hair cells. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What happens to the otoliths when moving upward (elevator movement) versus downward?","choices":["Otoliths only respond to side-to-side tilting, never vertical movement, since gravity acts uniformly on elevator direction","Moving upward: otoliths lift off the macular surface; moving downward: otoliths press down onto the macular surface","Otoliths remain completely stationary regardless of vertical movement, since their mass is too great to shift with brief accelerations","Moving upward: otoliths press down on the macular surface; moving downward: otoliths lift off the macular surface"],"answer":3,"exp":"When moving upward, the otoliths press down on the macular surface; when moving downward, the otoliths lift off the macular surface. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which semicircular duct is associated with the \"yes\" head-nodding plane, and which with the \"no\" head-shaking plane?","choices":["All three ducts detect identical head movements","Lateral semicircular duct: \"yes\"; posterior semicircular duct: \"no\"","Posterior semicircular duct: \"yes\"; anterior semicircular duct: \"no\"","Anterior semicircular duct: \"yes\"; lateral semicircular duct: \"no\""],"answer":3,"exp":"The anterior semicircular duct is associated with the \"yes\" (nodding) plane of head movement, the lateral semicircular duct with the \"no\" (shaking) plane, and the posterior semicircular duct with \"tilting the head.\" — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Why does spinning in a circle and then stopping abruptly cause dizziness, per the material?","choices":["Dizziness occurs only in the cochlea, unrelated to the vestibular system, resulting from residual sound-induced hair cell fatigue","Perilymph (not endolymph) is responsible for this dizziness effect, continuing to swirl within the bony labyrinth after stopping","The endolymph immediately stops moving the instant the body stops, causing a brief pressure spike the brain misreads as motion","The endolymph continues moving back and forth after stopping, causing the hair cells to bend back and forth, resulting in confusing signals"],"answer":3,"exp":"When you move in a circle and then stop abruptly, the endolymph continues moving back and forth, causing the hair cells to bend back and forth, resulting in confusing signals and thus dizziness. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the modiolus, and what does it contain?","choices":["A structure found in the vestibule, unrelated to the cochlea, that anchors the utricle and saccule to the temporal bone","A membrane separating the scala vestibuli and scala tympani, formed from a thin layer of connective tissue","The outermost layer of the cochlea; contains no neural structures, serving purely as a protective bony shell","The central area around which the cochlea's spirals coil; contains sensory neurons associated with CN VIII"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cochlea consists of \"snail-shaped\" spirals that coil around a central area called the modiolus; within the modiolus are sensory neurons associated with CN VIII. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What are the three layers (ducts) of each cochlear spiral, and what fluid does each contain?","choices":["Only two layers exist: scala vestibuli and scala tympani, both containing endolymph throughout","All three layers contain identical fluid, allowing free exchange of ions between each duct","Vestibular duct (endolymph), tympanic duct (endolymph), and cochlear duct (perilymph)","Scala vestibuli (perilymph), scala tympani (perilymph), and scala media (endolymph, located between the other two)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Each cochlear spiral consists of three layers: the scala vestibuli (vestibular duct, containing perilymph), the scala tympani (tympanic duct, containing perilymph), and the scala media (cochlear duct, containing endolymph), located between the scala vestibuli and scala tympani. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Where are the scala vestibuli and scala tympani connected, and where do sense organs rest?","choices":["Sense organs rest directly on the modiolus, not the basilar membrane, and the ducts connect at the round window","Not connected at any point, since the scala vestibuli and scala tympani remain entirely separate fluid systems","Connected at the apical end of the cochlea; sense organs rest on the basilar membrane within the scala media","Connected only at the cochlea's base; sense organs instead rest within the scala tympani"],"answer":2,"exp":"The scala vestibuli and scala tympani are connected at the apical end of the cochlea; sense organs rest on the basilar membrane within the scala media. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Where does the Organ of Corti (spiral organ) rest, and what membrane are its hair cells in contact with?","choices":["Rests on the basilar membrane between the scala media and scala tympani; hair cells are in contact with the overlying tectorial membrane","Rests on the tectorial membrane; hair cells contact the basilar membrane on the opposite side","Rests within the vestibule, unrelated to the cochlea, anchored to the utricle's macula","Rests on the oval window directly, with hair cells contacting the stapes footplate"],"answer":0,"exp":"The Organ of Corti (spiral organ) rests on the basilar membrane between the scala media and the scala tympani; its hair cells are in contact with the overlying tectorial membrane, which is attached to the lining of the scala media. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What neurotransmitter mediates parasympathetic stimulation of the heart, and what is its overall effect?","choices":["Norepinephrine; increases heart rate through beta-1 adrenergic receptor stimulation","Dopamine; only affects blood pressure, not heart rate directly","Epinephrine; has no effect on heart rate, acting solely on vascular smooth muscle","Acetylcholine; opposes the sympathetic nervous system and slows heart rate via vagal tone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Parasympathetic nervous system stimulation of the heart is mediated by acetylcholine, opposing the sympathetic nervous system; parasympathetic input dominates the autonomic stimulation at rest, slowing heart rate via vagal tone, and the vagus nerve controlling this arises from the vagal nuclei in the brainstem. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Besides controlling heart rate, what other sensory function does the vagus nerve serve for the heart?","choices":["Has no sensory function related to the heart","Produces atrial natriuretic peptide","Regulates coronary blood flow directly via smooth muscle contraction","Senses pain for the heart"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vagus nerve also senses pain for the heart, in addition to its parasympathetic motor role slowing heart rate. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What effect does sympathetic innervation have on the heart, and from which spinal cord segments does it arise?","choices":["Increases the rate and force of heart contractions; arises from T1-T5 spinal cord segments","Decreases the rate and force of contractions; arises from T1-T5 spinal cord segments via the vagus nerve","Has no effect on heart rate, only force; arises from lumbar segments","Increases contraction force only; arises from the sacral spinal cord segments"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sympathetic innervation increases both the rate and the force of heart contractions, arising from the T1-T5 segments of the spinal cord; preganglionic fibers enter the sympathetic trunk and ascend through ganglia, and postganglionic fibers travel through the heart via cardiac nerves. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What do coronary blood vessels supply, and where do the major coronary arteries branch from?","choices":["Supply blood to the lungs; branch off the pulmonary trunk near the pulmonary valve","Supply blood to the brain; branch off the aortic arch's carotid branches","Supply blood to the kidneys; branch off the abdominal aorta below the renal hilum","Supply blood to the heart muscles; branch off the ascending aorta near its base"],"answer":3,"exp":"Coronary blood vessels supply blood to the heart muscles, with the major coronary vessels (right and left coronary arteries) branching off the ascending aorta near its base. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Where does the right coronary artery (RCA) pass, and what are its major branches?","choices":["Passes through the interventricular septum exclusively, giving rise only to small septal perforating branches that feed the conduction system","Has no described branches at all, running as a single continuous, unbranching vessel around the right atrioventricular groove toward the apex","Passes between the right auricle and right ventricle; major branches include atrial branches, right marginal branch, posterior interventricular branch, and conducting system branches","Passes between the left atrium and left ventricle; branches only to the left ventricle's posterior wall via its circumflex continuation near the groove"],"answer":2,"exp":"The right coronary artery (RCA) passes between the right auricle and right ventricle; its major branches include atrial branches, the right marginal branch, the posterior interventricular branch, and conducting system branches. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"In what percentage of individuals does the RCA supply the SA node, and what does the RCA supply in general regarding the AV node?","choices":["RCA supplies the SA node in 100% of patients, with no anatomical variation described in the population","RCA supplies the AV node only 10% of the time, with the LCA supplying it in the remaining 90%","RCA never supplies the SA node; only the LCA does, via its circumflex branch in all patients","RCA supplies the SA node in 60% of patients (LCA via circumflex branch in 40%); RCA supplies the AV node"],"answer":3,"exp":"The RCA supplies the SA node in 60% of patients (with the LCA supplying it via the circumflex branch in the remaining 40%); the RCA supplies the AV node. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"In what percentage of individuals does the posterior interventricular branch arise from the RCA versus the LCA?","choices":["50/50 split with no variation","67-85% from the LCA; 15-33% from the RCA","100% from the RCA, with no LCA contribution","67-85% from the RCA; 15-33% from the LCA"],"answer":3,"exp":"The posterior interventricular branch arises from the RCA in 67-85% of individuals, and from the LCA in 15-33% of individuals. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What are the major branches of the left coronary artery (LCA)?","choices":["Posterior interventricular branch only, arising directly from the main trunk of the left coronary artery","Atrial branches only, with no ventricular branches, supplying solely the left atrial wall","Only the right marginal branch, extending from the LCA toward the right atrioventricular groove","Circumflex branch (forming the left marginal branch and posterior left ventricular branch) and anterior interventricular branch"],"answer":3,"exp":"The major branches of the left coronary artery are the circumflex branch (which branches to form the left marginal branch and the posterior left ventricular branch) and the anterior interventricular branch (which connects to the posterior interventricular branch via anastomoses). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What supplies the interventricular septum, and what supplies the anterolateral versus posteromedial papillary muscles?","choices":["Neither papillary muscle receives coronary blood supply, relying instead on diffusion from ventricular blood","Interventricular septum is mostly LCA; anterolateral papillary muscle is supplied by LCA branches, posteromedial by the RCA's posterior interventricular branch","Interventricular septum is mostly RCA; both papillary muscles are supplied by the LCA's circumflex branch","The interventricular septum receives no coronary blood supply at all, relying solely on endocardial diffusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"The interventricular septum is mostly supplied by the LCA; the anterolateral papillary muscle is supplied by the left anterior interventricular branch and circumflex branch (both LCA), while the posteromedial papillary muscle is supplied by the posterior interventricular branch (RCA). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What are the named cardiac veins, and where do they all eventually drain?","choices":["All cardiac veins drain into the left atrium, delivering deoxygenated blood directly into systemic circulation rather than returning it to the right heart","Great cardiac vein, middle cardiac vein, posterior vein of the left ventricle, small cardiac vein, and anterior cardiac veins; all eventually lead to the coronary sinus, which drains into the right atrium","Only one cardiac vein exists: the great cardiac vein, which drains directly into the inferior vena cava without passing through the coronary sinus at all","All cardiac veins drain directly into the aorta, mixing deoxygenated blood with oxygenated systemic blood before it reaches the peripheral tissues"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cardiac veins include the great cardiac vein, middle cardiac vein, posterior vein of the left ventricle, small cardiac vein, and anterior cardiac veins; all of these eventually lead to the coronary sinus, which drains into the right atrium. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Where does the ascending aorta begin, and what are its only branches?","choices":["Begins at the aortic arch; branches include the carotid arteries supplying the head and neck","Begins at the aortic valve; the left and right coronary arteries are its only branches","Begins at the AV node; branches to the ventricles via the interventricular septum","Begins at the pulmonary valve; has no branches until it reaches the aortic arch"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ascending aorta begins at the aortic valve; the left and right coronary arteries, which branch off its base, are the only branches of the ascending aorta. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What three vessels does the aortic arch branch into?","choices":["Right and left coronary arteries only, supplying the myocardium before the arch continues distally","Superior and inferior vena cava, draining directly into the arch's concave inner curvature","Brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid artery, and left subclavian artery","Pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries, carrying deoxygenated blood toward the lungs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Blood in the aortic arch branches into the brachiocephalic trunk, the left common carotid artery, and the left subclavian artery (with the aortic arch continuing on as the descending aorta). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What is the hematocrit (packed cell volume), and what are the normal male versus female values?","choices":["The total blood volume in liters, not a percentage; males average ~5.5 L, females ~4.5 L","The percentage of whole blood occupied by formed elements; males ~45% (40-54%), females ~42% (37-47%)","The percentage of plasma in whole blood, identical for males and females at approximately 55%","A measure of blood pH, not cell volume; normal range is 7.35-7.45 for both sexes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hematocrit (also called packed cell volume or volume of packed red cells) is the percentage of whole blood occupied by the formed elements; normal values are approximately 45% (40-54%) for males and 42% (37-47%) for females. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What is the structure of a red blood cell (RBC), and what does it contain?","choices":["A cell shaped like a sphere with a thick central region, retaining a nucleus and mitochondria for its own independent energy production","A cell containing melanin instead of hemoglobin, produced and stored within melanocytes located deep within the bone marrow stroma","A cell with a large, multilobed nucleus and abundant cytoplasmic organelles, structurally similar to a circulating neutrophil","Biconcave disc shape, lacking organelles and a nucleus (anucleated), containing hemoglobin (4 polypeptide subunits: 2 alpha, 2 beta chains, each with heme containing iron)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Red blood cells are biconcave discs with a thin central region, lacking cell organelles and a nucleus (anucleated); they contain hemoglobin, consisting of 4 polypeptide subunits (2 alpha chains and 2 beta chains), each chain containing heme, which contains iron. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What are the two major classes of leukocytes, and which cell types belong to each?","choices":["Erythrocytes and platelets, which are the two major leukocyte classes","Granulocytes (neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils) and agranulocytes (monocytes, lymphocytes)","Only one class exists: granulocytes","Granulocytes (monocytes, lymphocytes) and agranulocytes (neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The two major classes of leukocytes are granulocytes (granular leukocytes: neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils) and agranulocytes (agranular leukocytes: monocytes, lymphocytes), five major types total. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What do neutrophil granules contain, and how long do neutrophils typically live?","choices":["Chemicals to kill bacteria; live about 10 hours","Histamine and heparin; live several years","No granules at all; live indefinitely","Chemicals to reduce inflammation; live about 10 days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Neutrophil granules contain chemicals to kill bacteria; neutrophils are typically the first WBC at a bacterial site, are very active phagocytic cells, and only live about 10 hours. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What do eosinophil granules release, and what do eosinophils typically attack?","choices":["Release chemicals that reduce inflammation; attack foreign substances that have reacted with circulating antibodies (e.g., allergic reactions, parasites)","Release no granule chemicals at all; function only in blood clotting alongside platelets during vascular injury and wound repair","Release histamine and heparin granules; attack bacteria directly through active phagocytosis and enzymatic digestion","Release chemicals to attract fibroblasts to wound sites for repair; attack cancer cells via direct cytotoxic granule release mechanisms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Eosinophil granules release chemicals that reduce inflammation; eosinophils attack foreign substances that have reacted with circulating antibodies, such as in allergic reactions or parasitic infections, and typically have a bilobed nucleus. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What do basophil granules release, and what do histamine and heparin each do?","choices":["Release chemicals that constrict blood vessels and actively promote blood clotting","Release antibodies, functioning identically to plasma cells in the immune response","Release histamine (dilates blood vessels) and heparin (prevents abnormal blood clotting)","Release chemicals that kill bacteria only, with no other described granule functions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Basophil granules release histamine, which dilates blood vessels, and heparin, which prevents abnormal blood clotting; the nucleus is usually hidden due to all the granules. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What is distinctive about monocytes, in terms of size, nucleus shape, and function?","choices":["Cells identical in size and function to platelets, releasing clotting factors into the surrounding bloodstream","Small, non-phagocytic cells with a multilobed nucleus, releasing histamine and heparin from granules","Large phagocytic cells with a kidney-shaped or large oval-shaped nucleus, releasing chemicals to attract other phagocytic cells and fibroblasts","Cells that only produce antibodies, never phagocytosing bacteria or cellular debris, and lacking any kidney-shaped nucleus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Monocytes are large phagocytic cells with a kidney-shaped or large oval-shaped nucleus; they release chemicals to attract other phagocytic cells and to attract fibroblasts. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What are lymphocytes responsible for, and what three cell types can they differentiate into?","choices":["Primary cells of the lymphatic system, responsible for specific immunity; differentiate into T cells, B cells, and NK cells","Cells found only in bone marrow tissue, differentiating into osteocytes and osteoblasts over time","Primary phagocytic cells of the blood; they differentiate directly into neutrophils and eosinophils over time","Cells involved only in blood clotting, differentiating directly into platelets within the bloodstream"],"answer":0,"exp":"Lymphocytes are the primary cells of the lymphatic system, responsible for specific immunity; they can differentiate to form T cells (attack foreign cells directly), B cells (secrete antibodies), and NK cells (immune surveillance). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"What are platelets derived from, and what is thrombocytopenia versus thrombocytosis?","choices":["Derived from megakaryocytes; thrombocytopenia is lower-than-normal platelet count, thrombocytosis is higher-than-normal platelet count","Platelets are not derived from any other cell type at all; thrombocytopenia and thrombocytosis both describe entirely normal counts","Thrombocytopenia and thrombocytosis both describe an identical, entirely normal platelet count in healthy adults","Derived directly from lymphocytes; thrombocytopenia is a higher-than-normal platelet count, thrombocytosis is lower"],"answer":0,"exp":"Platelets (formerly called thrombocytes) are derived from megakaryocytes, which fragment to form membrane-enclosed packets of chemicals (about 350,000 platelets per microliter of blood); thrombocytopenia is a lower-than-normal platelet count, while thrombocytosis is a higher-than-normal platelet count. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What three things does the lymphatic system consist of?","choices":["Only lymph and circulating blood plasma together","Lymph, lymphatic vessels, and lymphatic tissues and organs","Only lymphatic vessels, with no associated lymphoid organs at all","Erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lymphatic system consists of lymph, lymphatic vessels, and lymphatic tissues and organs. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What does lymph consist of?","choices":["Interstitial fluid, lymphocytes, and macrophages","Only dissolved plasma proteins present","Only platelets and fibrinogen present","Only red blood cells and clotting factors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Lymph consists of interstitial fluid, lymphocytes, and macrophages. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What are the primary lymphatic structures, and what are the secondary lymphatic structures?","choices":["Primary: tonsils and lymph nodes; Secondary: thymus, red bone marrow, and spleen","Both categories include identical structures, namely the thymus and red bone marrow","Primary: thymus and red bone marrow; Secondary: tonsils, lymph nodes, spleen, and MALT","Only primary lymphatic structures exist; there are no secondary structures described"],"answer":2,"exp":"Primary lymphatic structures are the thymus and red bone marrow; secondary lymphatic structures are the tonsils, lymph nodes (axillary, lumbar, pelvic, inguinal), spleen, and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"What are type I pneumocytes (alveolar cells), and where does gas exchange occur?","choices":["Cuboidal cells that secrete surfactant into the alveolar space","Cartilage cells that support and stiffen the alveolar wall","Squamous cells lining the alveoli, where gas exchange takes place","Macrophages that phagocytize debris within the alveolar space"],"answer":2,"exp":"Type I pneumocytes (alveolar cells) are the squamous cells lining the alveoli in which gas exchange takes place; the alveolar lining consists of a single layer of these squamous cells. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What do type II pneumocytes secrete, and what is the function of this secretion?","choices":["Secrete mucus, which traps and clears inhaled debris","Secrete hormones that regulate blood pressure","Secrete digestive enzymes for local protein breakdown","Secrete surfactant, which prevents alveolar collapse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Type II pneumocytes, scattered among the type I pneumocytes, secrete surfactant, which prevents alveolar collapse. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What are alveolar macrophages (dust cells), and what do they do?","choices":["Cartilage cells that provide rigid structural support within the bronchiole walls","Cells that move freely within the alveoli, phagocytizing bacteria, dead cells, and foreign bodies","Squamous cells that form the thin alveolar wall where gas exchange occurs","Cells that produce surfactant, preventing alveolar collapse during exhalation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Alveolar macrophages (dust cells) move freely within the alveoli, phagocytizing (engulfing and digesting) bacteria, dead cells, and foreign bodies. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is the sequence of gas exchange at the alveoli, from pulmonary arteries to pulmonary veins?","choices":["Only CO2 is exchanged at the alveoli; oxygen exchange instead occurs separately in the peripheral tissue capillaries, bypassing the alveolar sacs and pulmonary circulation entirely","Pulmonary arteries transport CO2 to alveolar capillaries → CO2 leaves capillaries into alveolar sacs → O2 leaves alveolar sacs into capillaries → O2 enters pulmonary veins, returning to the heart","Pulmonary veins transport CO2 to the alveolar capillaries → CO2 leaves into the alveolar sacs → O2 leaves the sacs into the veins → O2 enters the pulmonary arteries, returning to the heart","Gas exchange occurs directly within the pulmonary arteries themselves as blood travels toward the lungs, entirely bypassing the alveolar capillaries and sacs altogether"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pulmonary arteries transport carbon dioxide to the alveolar capillaries; carbon dioxide leaves the capillaries and enters the alveolar sacs; oxygen leaves the alveolar sacs and enters the capillaries; oxygen then enters the pulmonary veins and returns to the heart to be pumped to all parts of the body. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Approximately how many alveoli does each lung have, and what surrounds each alveolus to maintain its shape?","choices":["About 1,000 alveoli; cartilage rings surround each alveolus to maintain its rigid shape","About 150 million alveoli; elastic tissue surrounds each alveolus to maintain shape and position during breathing","About 150 million alveoli; no surrounding tissue maintains their shape, which instead relies on internal pressure alone","About 1 million alveoli; smooth muscle alone maintains their shape throughout the breathing cycle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each lung has about 150 million alveoli, surrounded by an extensive network of capillaries; elastic tissue surrounds each alveolus, maintaining its shape and position during inhalation and exhalation. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What do the bronchial arteries supply, and are the alveoli/respiratory bronchioles dependent on them for oxygen?","choices":["Bronchial arteries supply the entire lung uniformly, with no distinction made between the bronchi and the alveolar tissue","The alveoli depend entirely on the bronchial arteries for their oxygen supply, receiving none from inspired air directly","Bronchial arteries supply the bronchi and connective tissue of the lungs; the alveoli and respiratory bronchioles are self-sufficient at oxygenation","Bronchial arteries supply only the alveoli directly; the bronchi instead receive no dedicated blood supply of their own"],"answer":2,"exp":"The bronchial arteries supply the bronchi and connective tissue of the lungs; the alveoli and respiratory bronchioles are self-sufficient at oxygenation (via direct gas exchange with inspired air, not requiring bronchial artery-delivered blood for their own oxygen needs). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Where do the bronchial arteries originate from, and what is the most common anatomical variation?","choices":["Originate from the coronary arteries directly, near vertebral level T5-T6, with a fixed pattern of two vessels on each side","Originate from the abdominal aorta near vertebral level L1-L2; there is no described anatomical variation among individuals","Originate from the proximal descending thoracic aorta around T5-T6; 40% (majority) of the population have one right and two left bronchial arteries","Originate from the pulmonary trunk near its bifurcation; all individuals have an identical two right and two left bronchial artery pattern"],"answer":2,"exp":"The bronchial arteries originate from the proximal descending thoracic aorta, around the level of T5 or T6; there are four anatomical variations, with 40% (the majority) of the population having one right bronchial artery and two left bronchial arteries. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What is the visceral pleura, and what is the parietal pleura?","choices":["Visceral pleura covers the outer surface of the lung; parietal pleura covers the inside lining of the thoracic wall","Visceral pleura covers the inside of the thoracic wall; parietal pleura covers the outer surface of the lung","Neither pleural layer is a serous membrane; both consist instead of dense fibrous connective tissue","Both pleural layers cover identical structures, each lining only the outer surface of the lungs directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"The visceral pleura portion covers the outer surface of the lung, while the parietal pleura portion covers the inside lining of the thoracic wall; together they form the continuous serous membrane lining each lung. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What separates the right and left pleural cavities, and how much pleural fluid does the pleural cavity normally contain?","choices":["Separated by the diaphragm; contains 500 mL of pleural fluid","Separated by the sternum; contains no fluid normally present","Not separated at all; they form one continuous cavity","Separated by the mediastinum; contains 5-10 mL of pleural fluid"],"answer":3,"exp":"The right and left pleural cavities are separated by the mediastinum; the pleural cavity between the visceral and parietal pleura normally contains 5-10 mL of pleural fluid (serous fluid). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What is the function of pleural fluid?","choices":["Produces surfactant for the alveoli during each breath","Reduces friction when the lungs move upon inhalation and exhalation","Has no functional role; it is simply a passive metabolic byproduct","Transports oxygen directly into the underlying lung tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pleural fluid reduces friction when the lungs move during inhalation and exhalation. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What are the primary respiratory muscles for inhalation, and what nerve innervates the diaphragm?","choices":["The vagus nerve innervates the diaphragm and the internal intercostals","Diaphragm (innervated by the phrenic nerve, C3-C5) and external intercostals","Internal intercostals and the sternocleidomastoid muscles only","Only accessory muscles exist, with no primary muscles for inhalation"],"answer":1,"exp":"The primary respiratory muscles are the diaphragm (innervated by the phrenic nerve, C3-C5) and the external intercostals; internal intercostals are also involved in respiration (for exhalation). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"What do the diaphragm's contraction and relaxation cause, respectively?","choices":["The diaphragm's movement has no measurable effect on overall breathing mechanics or lung volume changes","Contraction (diaphragm moves superiorly) causes exhalation; relaxation (moves inferiorly) causes inhalation","Both contraction and relaxation cause identical increases in thoracic cavity volume each time","Contraction (diaphragm moves inferiorly) causes inhalation; relaxation (diaphragm moves superiorly) causes exhalation"],"answer":3,"exp":"The diaphragm contracts (moving inferiorly) to cause inhalation, and relaxes (moving superiorly) to cause exhalation. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 79"}]};
